<p>Italian voters rejected Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni’s proposed judicial overhaul in a referendum, with the “No” side winning about 54% of the vote and “Yes” taking about 46%, according to near-final and final tallies reported by multiple outlets. The package included a proposal to select members of the High Council of the Judiciary by sortition, and the outcome handed Meloni’s government a major political setback. Meloni said she would respect the result and keep governing through the end of her mandate in 2027, while calling the outcome a missed chance to modernize Italy. Several outlets said the campaign had turned into a broader test of confidence in her government, just a year before the next national election.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/H2M7m0FbDnWHXvLfZmMGIhuhix-irP60tK4ZNTugNZUW-n19-rsI1LvmZPxezQhQdFHdLkUQ6mmmGt0f_KlY_DUZ3zP99Dvl_1LPDTKHxNAD' alt='Italian Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni looks on ahead of a meeting with Austrian Chancellor Christian Stocker in Rome, Italy, on July 15, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Campaign stakes: Although Meloni argued that the vote was not about her leadership, she campaigned hard alongside her coalition partners, while opposition parties rallied behind the “No” campaign.</li><li>Turnout and weight: Le Monde reported high voter turnout, reinforcing the sense that the result reflected a meaningful national judgment rather than a narrow institutional dispute.</li><li>Stable government: Despite the referendum defeat, Meloni still leads a coalition that several reports described as unusually stable by recent Italian standards.</li></ul><blockquote>Italian citizens have decided and we respect their decision as always, - Giorgia Meloni</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Opposition parties: Opposition forces campaigned for a “No” vote and framed the referendum as a defense of judicial independence, not a technical institutional tweak. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1984773/blow-to-meloni-as-she-suffers-referendum-defeat'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Daniele Albertazzi: The University of Surrey politics professor said the outcome showed Meloni had lost the electorate on a key manifesto issue. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1984773/blow-to-meloni-as-she-suffers-referendum-defeat'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Giovanni Bachelet: A leader of the successful “No” campaign described the result as a defense of the constitutional order and part of Italy’s longer pattern of resisting attempts to rewrite foundational rules through short-term political mandates. (<a href='https://jacobin.com/2026/03/meloni-italy-justice-reform-referendum/'>Jacobin</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/mar/23/local-eu-elections-slovenia-italy-france-latest-updates-news'>Giorgia Meloni concedes defeat in Italy’s judiciary reform referendum – as it happened</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3347636/italian-voters-reject-judicial-reform-setback-pm-giorgia-meloni'>Italian voters reject judicial reform in setback for Meloni</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.economist.com/europe/2026/03/23/giorgia-melonis-big-electoral-setback-in-italy'>Giorgia Meloni’s big electoral setback in Italy</a> - economist.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/23/world/europe/italy-referendum-judiciary-meloni.html'>Italians Reject Judicial Overhaul, Undermining Meloni</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/crl1ne8dj1eo'>Referendum defeat leaves Italy&#x27;s Meloni looking more vulnerable</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/italy-s-meloni-concedes-defeat-on-judiciary-referendum/a-76494232'>Italy&#x27;s Meloni concedes defeat on judiciary referendum</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/24/in-italy-giorgia-meloni-s-lost-referendum-opens-a-new-chapter-in-her-term_6751753_4.html'>In Italy, Giorgia Meloni&#x27;s 